well let's go straight to our deputy  political editor sam coates who is uh in  downing street outside number 10. uh sam  already rumors uh flying around what do  we know  well we won't get confirmation until a  little bit late this afternoon when we  see cabinet ministers about to be  appointed in post walking up the famous  street uh but at the moment we're just  in the slightly more gruesome phase of a  cabinet reshuffle the sackings we don't  know who it's going to be but as we  speak boris johnson has returned after  pmq's to his office behind the speaker's  chair and that's where he will hold the  more difficult conversations with  members of his top team some of whom  will no longer be members of his top  team in the first big reshuffle of his  cabinet in 18 months so what do we  expect well there are rumors about all  corners of the cabinet including the  great offices of state will pretty patel  remain home secretary after all the  criticism of the way that the home  office has handled the migrant boat  situation boats coming in over the  channel in increasingly large numbers  with her seemingly unable to stem the  flow will dominic raab remain the  foreign secretary the crisis in  afghanistan he acted like a lightning  rod for lots of criticism over over him  his uh  holiday the way he handled the situation  some in tory circles asking whether he  is suited to the foreign office he's the  first secretary of state he's a deputy  prime minister in albert name he stood  in for boris johnson last year when  boris johnson had to go to hospital with  covid but the criticism over the last  few weeks has been stinging will dominic  rob survive then other names in the top  tier of the cabinet gavin williamson  probably the most often mentioned the  embattled education secretary he's had  to deal with the difficult situation on  schools often with stumbling media  performances  and criticism about the decisions that  kind of back and forth the hokey cokey  about whether or not schools would  remain open through the pandemic much of  the decision making of course foisted  upon him by number 10 but nevertheless  widely expected not to remain in that  post the test for some in his own party  is whether or not he leaves government  altogether and is sacked because of what  some perceive as poor performance or  whether or not boris johnson who relied  on gavin williamson to get elected  actually survived in another role he  knows northern ireland very well could  he end up as the secretary of state  there other embattled ministers could  hang on or could go we're looking at  people like robert genwright reports  over the weekend that his flagship  housing policy looks likely to be  significantly watered down in the face  of opposition who could be the winners  uh well one name often mentioned is liz  trust currently the trade secretary  she's been doing the first of those  series of international trade deals most  notably with australia she's looking to  start work with america but she has been  very highly rated amongst the party  could she go up if a vacancy uh it does  uh open up that could be uh possibly  even the foreign office should boris  johnson look kindly upon her and then  there are all sorts of other questions  most notably one of the most mercurial  figures in government michael gove he is  the cabinet office minister which is a  minister in charge of the inner workings  of government huge amount of speculation  perhaps some of it driven by him and his  allies that he could end up with a big  promotion in this reshuffle maybe as  home secretary certainly we're here  reports that he's been sniffing around  police reform and other key areas but  could michael gove who famously stabbed  boris johnson in the front in 2016  really get a big promotion at this point  in time not least because just yesterday  in cabinet boris johnson handed michael  gave a bit of a new role making sure  that the shortages uh that the uh the  delays at the border the delays with hgv  drivers delivering goods to this country  and the shortages on the shelves aren't  going to continue to christmas so if  michael gove's getting new roles as  recently as yesterday will he be moving  up or will he be staying in that  slightly odd role as cabinet office  minister another long-standing minister  that we don't know the future of brandon  lewis he's in the northern ireland  secretary  big change in northern ireland obviously  affected uh by brexit you've got  political change there with the dep  tanking in the polls big questions of  what will happen in northern ireland in  the may elections where stormont goes to  the polls uh will boris johnson want to  reset the relationship uh over there or  has his work trying to calm tensions  that actually uh played off uh and boris  johnson decides to keep brandon lewis in  post uh one person two people who are  almost certain to be safe well there's  sanjay javid uh he's the new health  secretary the last time we were standing  in downing street doing a reshuffle it  was sanjay javid being sacked as  chancellor in a row curiously enough  about who should be his advisors that  was the height of the dominic cummings  era now sajid javid back in favor and  given that he's only been in post a few  weeks after the departure of matt  hancock he is almost certain to stay  another person probably feeling more  comfortable than most uh well that is  soella braverman she was only  reappointed  as attorney general last week after  coming back off maternity leave so  unless she gets promoted it would be  very strange indeed for her not to stay  in her job there are other cabinet  figures that have been the subject of  some speculation like jacob rees-mogg  he's currently the commons leader some  had thought he might go in as number two  in the treasury which of course is a  cabinet level uh job that could uh free  up that commons leader job another  alternative uh for gavin williamson so  all sorts of speculation today the  question is what does boris johnson  think the aim of his reshuffle is he is  in a strong position at the moment he's  just carried out long delayed reforms to  social care he's about to have his party  confidence he will have been stung by  some of the the criticism that his  cabinet can be quite low powered so  looking according to that statement that  you just talked about uh to refresh his  top team to make it look like he's on  the front foot just to the point where  the polls uh suggest the conservatives  are enjoying a little bit of a dip  after those tax rises that he announced  so he's looking at a new cabinet in  order to go forward to the next  political season who could be the  winners uh well some of the names that  have been in the frame to go up in this  reshuffle figures like the minister  kemi badenock she had been tipped  for the education brief she has played  quite a strong role in the culture war  narrative of the last few months but  also is highly regarded inside downing  street former spectator employee she's  tipped to go up  victoria atkins uh said to have  impressed she just got a little bit  added to her brief looking after uh  afghan refugees when they come to the uk  uh to resettle uh another name that  could uh could go up uh oliver dowden is  the culture minister could he uh often  often see as a backroom figure actually  move into a bigger brief uh after uh  tending that uh culture media sport and  digital role uh one of the uh births for  him could be the education department  and then there's the question really of  who gets sacked all together from  cabinet uh if you need to bring people  in that you have to get people out of  your top task that will be the job that  boris johnson is doing as we speak and  then in a few hours we will learn the  winners

